---
title: 2-Dimensional Rosenbrock Function Nonlinear Optimization Benchmark
author: Chris Rackauckas
---

This is a test of the 2-dimensional Rosenbrock function, a standard nonlinear optimization test case.

```julia
# Define the problem to solve
using Optimization, ForwardDiff, Zygote, BenchmarkTools

rosenbrock(x, p) = (p[1] - x[1])^2 + p[2] * (x[2] - x[1]^2)^2
x0 = zeros(2)
_p = [1.0, 100.0]

f = OptimizationFunction(rosenbrock, Optimization.AutoForwardDiff())
l1 = rosenbrock(x0, _p)
prob = OptimizationProblem(f, x0, _p)
```

## Optim.jl Solvers

```julia
using OptimizationOptimJL
```

Start with some derivative-free optimizers

```julia
@btime sol = solve(prob, SimulatedAnnealing())
```

```julia
prob = OptimizationProblem(f, x0, _p, lb = [-1.0, -1.0], ub = [0.8, 0.8])
@btime sol = solve(prob, SAMIN())
```

```julia
l1 = rosenbrock(x0, _p)
prob = OptimizationProblem(rosenbrock, x0, _p)
@btime sol = solve(prob, NelderMead())
```

Now a gradient-based optimizer with forward-mode automatic differentiation

```julia
optf = OptimizationFunction(rosenbrock, Optimization.AutoForwardDiff())
prob = OptimizationProblem(optf, x0, _p)
@btime sol = solve(prob, BFGS())
```

Now a second order optimizer using Hessians generated by forward-mode automatic differentiation

```julia
@btime sol = solve(prob, Newton())
```

Now a second order Hessian-free optimizer

```julia
@btime sol = solve(prob, Optim.KrylovTrustRegion())
```

Now derivative-based optimizers with various constraints

```julia
cons = (res, x, p) -> res .= [x[1]^2 + x[2]^2]
optf = OptimizationFunction(rosenbrock, Optimization.AutoForwardDiff(); cons = cons)

prob = OptimizationProblem(optf, x0, _p, lcons = [-Inf], ucons = [Inf])
@btime sol = solve(prob, IPNewton()) # Note that -Inf < x[1]^2 + x[2]^2 < Inf is always true
```

```julia
prob = OptimizationProblem(optf, x0, _p, lcons = [-5.0], ucons = [10.0])
@btime sol = solve(prob, IPNewton()) # Again, -5.0 < x[1]^2 + x[2]^2 < 10.0
```

```julia
prob = OptimizationProblem(optf, x0, _p, lcons = [-Inf], ucons = [Inf],
    lb = [-500.0, -500.0], ub = [50.0, 50.0])
@btime sol = solve(prob, IPNewton())
```

```julia
prob = OptimizationProblem(optf, x0, _p, lcons = [0.5], ucons = [0.5],
    lb = [-500.0, -500.0], ub = [50.0, 50.0])
@btime sol = solve(prob, IPNewton()) # Notice now that x[1]^2 + x[2]^2 ≈ 0.5:
# cons(sol.u, _p) = 0.49999999999999994

function con_c(res, x, p)
    res .= [x[1]^2 + x[2]^2]
end

optf = OptimizationFunction(rosenbrock, Optimization.AutoForwardDiff(); cons = con_c)
prob = OptimizationProblem(optf, x0, _p, lcons = [-Inf], ucons = [0.25^2])
@btime sol = solve(prob, IPNewton()) # -Inf < con_c(sol.u, _p) = 0.25^2
```

```julia
function con2_c(res, x, p)
    res .= [x[1]^2 + x[2]^2, x[2] * sin(x[1]) - x[1]]
end

optf = OptimizationFunction(rosenbrock, Optimization.AutoForwardDiff(); cons = con2_c)
prob = OptimizationProblem(optf, x0, _p, lcons = [-Inf, -Inf], ucons = [Inf, Inf])
@btime sol = solve(prob, IPNewton())
```

Now let's switch over to OptimizationOptimisers with reverse-mode AD

```julia
using OptimizationOptimisers
optf = OptimizationFunction(rosenbrock, Optimization.AutoZygote())
prob = OptimizationProblem(optf, x0, _p)
@btime sol = solve(prob, OptimizationOptimisers.Adam(0.05), maxiters = 1000, progress = false)
```

Try out CMAEvolutionStrategy.jl's evolutionary methods

```julia
using OptimizationCMAEvolutionStrategy
@btime sol = solve(prob, CMAEvolutionStrategyOpt())
```

Now try a few NLopt.jl solvers with symbolic differentiation via ModelingToolkit.jl

```julia
using OptimizationNLopt, ModelingToolkit
optf = OptimizationFunction(rosenbrock, Optimization.AutoSymbolics())
prob = OptimizationProblem(optf, x0, _p)

@btime sol = solve(prob, Opt(:LN_BOBYQA, 2))
```

```julia
@btime sol = solve(prob, Opt(:LD_LBFGS, 2))
```

Add some box constraints and solve with a few NLopt.jl methods

```julia
prob = OptimizationProblem(optf, x0, _p, lb = [-1.0, -1.0], ub = [0.8, 0.8])
@btime sol = solve(prob, Opt(:LD_LBFGS, 2))
```

## Evolutionary.jl Solvers

```julia
using OptimizationEvolutionary
@btime sol = solve(prob, CMAES(μ = 40, λ = 100), abstol = 1e-15) # -1.0 ≤ x[1], x[2] ≤ 0.8
```

## BlackBoxOptim.jl Solvers

```julia
using OptimizationBBO
prob = Optimization.OptimizationProblem(rosenbrock, x0, _p, lb = [-1.0, -1.0],
    ub = [0.8, 0.43])
@btime sol = solve(prob, BBO_adaptive_de_rand_1_bin()) # -1.0 ≤ x[1] ≤ 0.8, 0.2 ≤ x[2] ≤ 0.43
```

## SimpleBFGS vs. Optim BFGS

Both solvers use the unconstrained Rosenbrock objective above, `Vector{Float64}` inputs,
the same parameters, `AutoForwardDiff()`, and a 1,000-iteration budget. The analytic
minimum is zero at `(1, 1)` because both squared terms vanish there. We set the
absolute gradient infinity-norm tolerance to `tol` via `abstol` for SimpleBFGS and
`g_abstol` for Optim BFGS. Optim's step and objective change tolerances are disabled.
A run meets the common target when the final objective error and gradient infinity
norm are both at most `tol`. The table reports this check alongside the solver's return code.

The timing is the median over warmed `solve` calls, with a fresh starting vector prepared
outside each sample. Allocation counts and bytes come from the same BenchmarkTools trial.
A separate solve counts every call to the objective, including calls on ForwardDiff dual
inputs. Each dual call evaluates the full two-variable gradient and contributes to both
counts. The final accuracy check runs separately. SimpleBFGS uses SimpleBroyden without
a line search, with one initial gradient evaluation and one per iteration. Its iteration
count comes from those calls.
Optim BFGS uses its default line search and supplies its own iteration count.
[SimpleBFGS implementation](https://github.com/SciML/Optimization.jl/blob/SimpleOptimization-v2.0.2/lib/SimpleOptimization/src/SimpleOptimization.jl)
[SimpleBroyden implementation](https://github.com/SciML/NonlinearSolve.jl/blob/SimpleNonlinearSolve-v2.14.3/lib/SimpleNonlinearSolve/src/broyden.jl)

```julia, wrap = false
using SimpleOptimization: SimpleBFGS
using Printf: @printf, @sprintf

function measure_rosenbrock_bfgs(start, p, solver, options, tol, minimum_value)
    maxiters = 1000
    ad = Optimization.AutoForwardDiff()
    prob = OptimizationProblem(OptimizationFunction(rosenbrock, ad), copy(start), p)
    sol = solve(prob, solver; maxiters, options...)
    trial = @benchmark solve(sample_prob, $solver; maxiters = $maxiters, $options...) setup = (
        sample_prob = remake($prob; u0 = copy($start))
    ) evals = 1 seconds = 0.25
    timing = BenchmarkTools.median(trial)

    objective_evals, gradient_evals = Ref(0), Ref(0)
    function counted_objective(x, p)
        objective_evals[] += 1
        if eltype(x) <: ForwardDiff.Dual
            gradient_evals[] += 1
        end
        return rosenbrock(x, p)
    end
    counted_f = OptimizationFunction(counted_objective, ad)
    counted_prob = OptimizationProblem(counted_f, copy(start), p)
    counted_sol = solve(counted_prob, solver; maxiters, options...)
    @assert isequal(sol.u, counted_sol.u) && sol.retcode == counted_sol.retcode

    iterations = solver isa SimpleBFGS ? gradient_evals[] - 1 : sol.stats.iterations
    value = rosenbrock(sol.u, p)
    gradient_norm = maximum(abs, ForwardDiff.gradient(x -> rosenbrock(x, p), sol.u))
    return (;
        start = Tuple(start), tol, solver = nameof(typeof(solver)), sol.retcode,
        meets_target = abs(value - minimum_value) <= tol && gradient_norm <= tol,
        iterations, objective_evals = objective_evals[], gradient_evals = gradient_evals[],
        value, gradient_norm, time_us = timing.time / 1000, timing.allocs, timing.memory,
    )
end

rosenbrock_minimizer = [1.0, 1.0]
rosenbrock_minimum = 0.0
@assert rosenbrock(rosenbrock_minimizer, _p) == rosenbrock_minimum

bfgs_starts = (x0, [-1.2, 1.0], [0.8, 0.64], [1.2, 1.2])
bfgs_cases = [(; start, tol) for start in bfgs_starts for tol in (1.0e-6, 1.0e-10)]
bfgs_results = [
    measure_rosenbrock_bfgs(start, _p, solver, options, tol, rosenbrock_minimum)
        for (; start, tol) in bfgs_cases for (solver, options) in (
            (SimpleBFGS(), (; abstol = tol)),
            (Optim.BFGS(), (; g_abstol = tol, x_abstol = NaN, x_reltol = NaN, f_abstol = NaN, f_reltol = NaN)),
        )
]

@printf(
    "%14s %7s %10s %9s %6s %5s %7s %7s %11s %11s %10s %6s %8s\n",
    "Start", "Target", "Solver", "Retcode", "Met?", "Iters", "f eval", "∇f eval", "f(x)",
    "‖∇f‖∞", "Median μs", "Allocs", "Bytes"
)
for row in bfgs_results
    @printf(
        "%14s %7.0e %10s %9s %6s %5d %7d %7d %11.3e %11.3e %10.3f %6d %8d\n",
        string(row.start), row.tol, row.solver, row.retcode, row.meets_target ? "yes" : "no",
        row.iterations, row.objective_evals, row.gradient_evals, row.value, row.gradient_norm,
        row.time_us, row.allocs, row.memory
    )
end
```

The plot includes timings for every returned solve. Use the target check and return
code in the table to interpret the cost of runs that stalled or missed the target.

```julia
using Plots: Plots, scatter, scatter!

case_positions = 1:length(bfgs_cases)
case_labels = [@sprintf("%s\n%.0e", string(Tuple(start)), tol) for (; start, tol) in bfgs_cases]
time_plot = scatter(;
    xticks = (case_positions, case_labels), xlabel = "Starting point and tolerance",
    ylabel = "Median solve time (μs)", yscale = :log10, size = (820, 380), legend = :topright,
    left_margin = 5Plots.mm, bottom_margin = 5Plots.mm,
)
for (solver, offset, marker) in ((:SimpleBFGS, -0.08, :circle), (:BFGS, 0.08, :diamond))
    times = [row.time_us for row in bfgs_results if row.solver == solver]
    scatter!(time_plot, case_positions .+ offset, times; label = string(solver), markershape = marker)
end
time_plot
```

```julia
Threads.nthreads()
```

